module lib/hf/basis
  The Gaussian basis functions every other lib/hf module integrates over:
  how many components a shell has, their order, and the numbers that
  normalize them.

  An atomic orbital here is a contraction: a fixed sum of Gaussians
  $e^{-a r^2}$ (the primitives, one exponent $a$ each) centered on one atom
  and sharing one angular part $x^{l_x} y^{l_y} z^{l_z}$. A shell is every
  component with the same angular momentum $l = l_x + l_y + l_z$: one for s,
  three for p, six Cartesian ones for d. The basis set supplies the exponents
  and raw coefficients; this module turns them into coefficients under one
  rule, that every Cartesian component has self-overlap one, and records
  where PySCF's convention differs from it.

  The packs a molecule carries (`atoms`, `shells`, `prims`) and the column
  constants below are fixed.

bas_ncart  @d8801c55
  why   The integral codes size their per-shell blocks with it, and the AO
        count of a basis is its sum over shells.
  l     the shell's angular momentum: `0` for s, `1` for p, `2` for d

bas_nsph  @0c094c43
  why   A spherical shell drops the Cartesian combinations that are
        lower angular momentum (for d, $x^2 + y^2 + z^2$, which is an s
        function); PySCF's `mol.nao` counts these.
  l     the shell's angular momentum

bas_odfact  @136a3fe7
  why   The moments of a Gaussian, $\int x^{2k} e^{-2 a x^2} dx$, carry
        $(2k-1)!!$, so the normalization factors are built from it. The loop
        multiplies $1 \cdot 3 \cdot 5 \cdots (2k-1)$.
  k     half the power of the moment; `0` gives the empty product
  r     the product so far

bas_powers  @c20d3dbf
  why   Every integral routine loops over a shell's components in this order,
        and the order must be PySCF's for the matrices to line up with PySCF's
        [test "d and f components come in PySCF's cart_labels order"].
  l     the shell's angular momentum
  P     the table being filled: row `c` holds $(l_x, l_y, l_z)$ of component `c`
  row   the next row of P to fill
  lx    the power of $x$, counting down from l
  ly    the power of $y$, counting down from what lx leaves

bas_fc  @1d811539
  why   This library normalizes every Cartesian component to one; PySCF does
        not for d and higher. Comparing a Cartesian matrix with PySCF's, or
        handing one to it, needs this factor.
  lx    power of $x$
  ly    power of $y$
  lz    power of $z$
  l     the component's angular momentum, $l_x + l_y + l_z$

bas_nc  @9f610254
  why   With it, two primitives of the same shell have an overlap that does not
        depend on which component they are, so one contracted coefficient per
        primitive serves every component of the shell.
  lx    power of $x$
  ly    power of $y$
  lz    power of $z$

bas_nr  @77b31c13
  why   The part of a primitive's norm that depends on its exponent. Folded into
        the contracted coefficient by bas_contract, so the integral codes never
        normalize a primitive themselves.
  a     the primitive's exponent
  l     the shell's angular momentum

bas_prim_overlap  @11bf6a28
  why   bas_contract sums it over pairs of primitives to find the shell's
        self-overlap before the rescale.
  a     the first primitive's exponent
  b     the second primitive's exponent
  l     the shell's angular momentum, shared by both

bas_nao_cart  @bf12eb04
  why   The size of every matrix in the Cartesian AO basis: S, H, D, F.
  shells  the shell pack, one row per shell, `[nshell, SH_LEN]`
  n       the count so far

bas_nao_sph  @da934aea
  why   The size of the matrices after the Cartesian-to-spherical transform
        (lib/hf/cart2sph).
  shells  the shell pack
  n       the count so far

bas_ao_table  @1babfb64
  why   Codes that work AO by AO (the one-electron integrals, the checks against
        PySCF) look up an AO's shell, atom and powers here instead of walking
        the shells each time.
  shells  the shell pack
  T       the table being filled, `[nao_cart, AO_LEN]`
  row     the next row of T, the AO index
  l       the current shell's angular momentum
  P       that shell's powers, from bas_powers

bas_contract  @723bc623
  why   Basis-set files print their coefficients rounded, and for primitives
        that are not normalized; rescaling the whole shell makes the rounding
        harmless and matches what PySCF does, so the integrals agree with
        PySCF's.
  shells  the shell pack
  prims   the primitive pack: exponent and raw coefficient, `[nprim, PR_LEN]`
  cn      the result, one number per primitive across all shells
  l       the current shell's angular momentum
  p0      the row of prims where this shell's primitives start
  np      how many primitives the shell has
  ss      the shell's self-overlap with the raw coefficients
  scale   $1/\sqrt{ss}$, which brings that self-overlap to one

bas_rng  @86f9708e
  why   The randomized tests draw their shells from it, so a failing case is
        reproduced from its seed alone.
  st    the generator's state, one number, advanced in place

bas_moment_self_overlap  @fa7cf752
  why   A second way to compute a component's self-overlap, from 1-D moments one
        axis at a time, so the test of bas_contract does not check a formula
        against itself.
  s     the shell
  nc    the component factor bas_nc
  p     the sum of two exponents: the product of two Gaussians on one center is
        a Gaussian with this exponent
  mx    the 1-D moment along $x$; my and mz likewise
  tot   the double sum over primitive pairs

bas_random_shells  @ba2590cf
  why   Test shells of every angular momentum up to f, with exponents and signs
        that no real basis set is limited to.
  nsh     how many shells to make
  seed    the generator's starting state
  shells  the shells pack: one row per shell
  st      the generator's state
  total   the primitives laid out so far

shell_atom  @775a3118
  shells the shells table, one row per shell
  s      the shell's row

shell_l  @e7a75fde
  shells the shells table, one row per shell
  s      the shell's row

shell_cart0  @1c6f7dc2
  shells the shells table, one row per shell
  s      the shell's row

shell_sph0  @b0734359
  shells the shells table, one row per shell
  s      the shell's row
